{"step_index":7009,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T14:12:42Z","thinking":"The documentation clarifies the `scope: drive.file` setting restricts access to files created by the application itself, linked to user authorization. The `root_folder_id` parameter allows specifying a starting point for file operations other than the root directory. This suggests the application's file access is limited to a self-created subset, with the option to narrow the scope further by defining a specific starting folder.\n\n\nSetting the scope to `drive.file` and locking the `root_folder_id` appears to provide the strongest security, restricting the application's access only to files it creates within a specified folder. Generating the authorization link with this scope will visually demonstrate the limited access Google grants, which is critical for user trust. This aims to create a completely isolated backup environment.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"rclone authorize \"drive\" \"REDACTED_CLIENT_ID.apps.googleusercontent.redacted\" \"[REDACTED_SECRET]\" --drive-scope=\"drive.file\" --auth-no-open-browser","Cwd":"/home/azureuser","WaitMsBeforeAsync":3000,"toolAction":"Testing rclone authorize with drive.file scope","toolSummary":"rclone authorize drive.file"}}]}
