{"step_index":7028,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T14:16:01Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-25T14:16:01Z\nCompleted At: 2026-09-25T14:16:04Z\n\nThe command exited with code 1.\nOutput:\nERROR: (gcloud.resource-manager.org-policies.describe) [[REDACTED_ID]@developer.gserviceaccount.com] does not have permission to access projects instance [irak-mahmud-project:getOrgPolicy] (or it may not exist): Request had insufficient authentication scopes. This command is authenticated as [REDACTED_ID]@developer.gserviceaccount.com which is the active account specified by the [core/account] property.\r\n- '@type': type.googleapis.com/google.rpc.ErrorInfo\r\n  domain: googleapis.com\r\n  metadata:\r\n    method: google.cloud.orgpolicy.v1.OrgPolicy.GetOrgPolicy\r\n    service: cloudresourcemanager.googleapis.com\r\n  reason: ACCESS_TOKEN_SCOPE_INSUFFICIENT\r\n\r\nIf you are in a compute engine VM, it is likely that the specified scopes during VM creation are not enough to run this command.\r\nSee https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/access/service-accounts#accesscopesiam for more information about access scopes.\r\nSee https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/access/create-enable-service-accounts-for-instances#changeserviceaccountandscopes for how to update access scopes of the VM.\r\nERROR: (gcloud.org-policies.describe) PERMISSION_DENIED: Request had insufficient authentication scopes. This command is authenticated as [REDACTED_ID]@developer.gserviceaccount.com which is the active account specified by the [core/account] property.\r\n- '@type': type.googleapis.com/google.rpc.ErrorInfo\r\n  domain: googleapis.com\r\n  metadata:\r\n    method: google.cloud.orgpolicy.v2.OrgPolicy.GetPolicy\r\n    service: orgpolicy.googleapis.com\r\n  reason: ACCESS_TOKEN_SCOPE_INSUFFICIENT\r\n\r\nIf you are in a compute engine VM, it is likely that the specified scopes during VM creation are not enough to run this command.\r\nSee https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/access/service-accounts#accesscopesiam for more information about access scopes.\r\nSee https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/access/create-enable-service-accounts-for-instances#changeserviceaccountandscopes for how to update access scopes of the VM.\r\n\n"}
{"step_index":7029,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T14:16:04Z","thinking":"Examining the user's prompt, the core issue appears to be a longstanding service account policy problem. The phrase \"secret key not allowed by org\" suggests organizational restrictions are in place, likely related to IAM policies or security configurations. The context points towards a Google Cloud Platform (GCP) environment where organizational constraints are commonly applied, and the use of the default service account could exacerbate the problem if the default scopes are too restrictive.\n\n\nThe error message explicitly points to a constraint preventing service account key creation. This restriction, `iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation`, is now a default security measure in Google Cloud, favoring Workload Identity Federation.\n\nTo resolve this, the appropriate Organization Policy needs to be modified, depending on whether one has Organization Admin or Project Owner privileges, and if overrides are permitted. The relevant console section is under IAM & Admin -> Organization Policies.\n\n\nThe initial steps describe directly modifying the organization policy to disable key creation restrictions. However, this is contingent on having the necessary permissions and the policy not being locked at a higher organizational level. The scenario also raises the question of whether the user, Iraq Bhai, controls the organization or is subject to external organizational constraints. Addressing both scenarios, access to the GCP console is required to resolve this issue and to see if the policy can be edited.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation\" override organization policy google cloud console steps","toolAction":"Searching GCP documentation","toolSummary":"Search iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation override"}}]}
{"step_index":7030,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T14:16:10Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-25T14:16:10Z\nCompleted At: 2026-09-25T14:16:14Z\nThe search for \"\"iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation\" override organization policy google cloud console steps\" returned the following summary:\nTo override the `iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation` organization policy, you must have the **Organization Policy Administrator** role (`roles/orgpolicy.policyAdmin`) assigned at the **organization level**[1][2][3].\n\n### Important Prerequisites\n*   **Permissions:** You must have the `roles/orgpolicy.policyAdmin` role[1][4]. If you do not see this option, ensure you are selecting your **Organization** in the Google Cloud Console project picker, not a specific project[5][3].\n*   **Hierarchy:** If the policy is enforced at the organization level, you can typically override it for a specific project by creating an exception[1][6]. However, if the policy is configured to prevent overrides or is enforced strictly, you may need to consult your organization's administrator[7][8].\n\n### Steps to Override the Policy\n1.  **Navigate to Organization Policies:**\n    *   Go to the [Organization Policies](https://console.cloud.google.com/iam-admin/orgpolicies) page in the Google Cloud Console[1][9].\n    *   In the resource selector at the top-left, **select your Organization** (the top-most node in your hierarchy)[1][3].\n2.  **Find the Constraint:**\n    *   In the filter bar, search for `iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation`[10][1].\n    *   Click on the policy name to open its details page[1].\n3.  **Override the Policy for a Project:**\n    *   Click **Edit**[1][2].\n    *   Under **Policy source**, select **Override parent's policy**[11][1].\n    *   Change the enforcement setting from **Enforced** to **Not enforced** (or customize it to exclude specific projects if supported)[1][6].\n    *   Click **Save**[1][12].\n\n### If the Override Option is Grayed Out\nIf you have the correct roles but still cannot override the policy, it is likely because:\n*   **Strict Enforcement:** The organization policy may have been set at a level that explicitly forbids further overrides[7][8].\n*   **Missing Org-Level Permissions:** You might be trying to manage the policy at the project level instead of the organization level[5][3]. Ensure you have navigated to the organization's policy settings[1][3].\n\nIf you are still unable to make changes, you must contact your organization's Google Cloud Administrator to request a temporary exception or a change to the policy[8].\n\nSources:\n[1] [dev.to](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFSQ-Pfeeag_CrAv75hwnho2TjHqzhjv3dVIOiViesBWhd_4Fo5UxqOYltofSjSQ-IqrigyPUut5ONi9-kmA_QTbmL35n2tL1FELuuCkQbKOJyXdEsZfPJjjA4ruq5LL5CRsUkhtyb0mhWcyQktJIwbfWpZ5WQ0mS9jrMvuT7MtaKLrI53WXTlQr3THid4k-bYnxto=)\n[2] [dev.to](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFSQ-Pfeeag_CrAv75hwnho2TjHqzhjv3dVIOiViesBWhd_4Fo5UxqOYltofSjSQ-IqrigyPUut5ONi9-kmA_QTbmL35n2tL1FELuuCkQbKOJyXdEsZfPJjjA4ruq5LL5CRsUkhtyb0mhWcyQktJIwbfWpZ5WQ0mS9jrMvuT7MtaKLrI53WXTlQr3THid4k-bYnxto=)\n[3] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQELnQT97wk8V01qSEIkh4EajqL7C7dD_2cFLcgyPyY21qAKvtFrfpRPyopkTP47gMFShplbA6jjb7S2gonGyrSfdA3nM9sETgOk3p82iovdCnaNgJpgKKcwRr_HjEIUF9OoKSWyQCDhKDw3STxmRgojmXC1u2JyMWCcDADRx8yabou0MDH3yjVbusltdTolEoamYtEQLtUB0FmNi9FlktyJnQzB9ZX8)\n[4] [google.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEbfOOvdkstn8U329EiCfVaHD0wrh320mAg-b9SUrbog36a3KsLwN49xncxT0HY64qxlcBQyzCWW07DombfN6GTXjHXx9WbdDTwQ9QgL_MPnPvJvrDM_LTpJOCln7clgThgCWmbDgbAq3IUOCUf0MuDHsWOAMyV_7T7n4UFgXkkRJQ=)\n[5] [reddit.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFpy-9xx62n35pnOUt01oQwX8EqOE2_Xz3XGxdzKvshkNzSoNTOGDy-eV7oDJRtCPi14obAST0318WExZNsGdglU06JxxRPzODQTGVHAIAJm3lO-FlUswjgfrRImflB